L156@P3: Figure 3: Overview of GLUESTICK. (Top) A VLA model is pruned with a standard
L157@P3: algorithm (e.g., Wanda) to enforce 2:4 sparsity in linear layers. (Middle) Offline, we
L158@P3: compute the gap between the dense and pruned weights and extract dominant lost directions
L159@P3: via SVD, yielding lightweight corrections. (Bottom) At inference time, these correction
L160@P3: terms are applied alongside the pruned weights, effectively adding back lost signal.
L161@P3: together, this line of work suggests that while pruning has been validated on LLMs, weight
L162@P3: adjustments cannot be assumed to be benign in all contexts. In robotics, where VLAs must
L163@P3: balance efficiency with both task success and safety, the implications of pruning remain
L164@P3: unexplored. No prior work has examined how pruning affects VLA performance or how
L165@P3: such impacts might be recovered. Direct weight-space interventions for post-pruned VLAs
L166@P3: are similarly underexplored. In this paper, we address this gap by analyzing pruning’s effect
L167@P3: on VLA models and introducing a training-free recovery method that restores both success
L168@P3: and safety.
L169@P3: 3 GLUESTICK
L170@P3: In this section, we first present our surprising finding that pruning can cause catastrophic
L171@P3: degradation in VLA success and safety. We then introduce our new method, GLUESTICK
L172@P3: (sinGular vaLUE STIChing), which glues pruned VLAs back to high task success rates
L173@P3: and safe behaviors as in the original dense models. Additional details and pseudocode are
L174@P3: available in Appendix A.
L175@P3: 3.1 Impact of Pruning on VLA Models
L176@P3: Pruning has substantially reduced memory usage in language models, with minimal loss in
L177@P3: accuracy (see Section 2). At the same sparsity levels commonly used in LLMs, we observe
L178@P3: surprisingly different outcomes on popular VLAs. Representative VLAs such as OpenVLA (Kim et al., 2024) and NaVILA (Cheng et al., 2025a), when pruned with Magnitude
L179@P3-4: 4Preprint, September 2025.
L180@P4: or Wanda, drop in success rate to 0% (from 85.2% and 43.0%, respectively). These come
L181@P4: alongside a rise in their unsafe-episode rates; in the worst case OpenVLA increases from
L182@P4: 33.4% to 51.6%, and for NaVILA from 23.0% to 100.0% (see Table 1). These findings show
L183@P4: that text-validated pruning does not directly transfer to embodied control.
L184@P4: To explain why pruning degrades VLAs far more than LLMs, we ask whether their weightspace properties differ. In particular, because OpenVLA is based on the LLaMA-2-7B backbone, the language components are directly comparable layer by layer. Thus, we examine
L185@P4: the singular value spectra of matched layers, plotted in Figure 2. In language-only models
L186@P4: the spectra are more anisotropic, evidenced by a steep initial drop followed by a long tail,
L187@P4: which concentrates energy in a few dominant directions. This profile helps pruning, since
L188@P4: removing small coefficients mainly trims low-energy directions while leaving the principal
L189@P4: subspaces intact. In contrast, VLA layers show a noticeably flatter decay, indicating energy
L190@P4: spread across many directions. In this regime, even small-magnitude coefficients contribute
L191@P4: to important subspaces, so pruning discards useful signal distributed throughout the matrix.
L192@P4: Based on this insight, our method in the following section explores the recovery of this lost
L193@P4: information within the weight space.
L194@P4: 3.2 Gluesticking Pruned Models
L195@P4: The space of pruning configurations is combinatorial, making optimal selection of weights
L196@P4: to remove intractable. Heuristic methods such as Magnitude and Wanda sidestep the global
L197@P4: optimization by scoring individual weights and pruning by score. While simple and efficient,
L198@P4: these heuristics discard correlated weights under grouped sparsity constraints (e.g., 50%
L199@P4: sparsity with 2:4 or 4:8 groups), which could be especially harmful for VLA models.
L200@P4: We propose GLUESTICK, a post-hoc, training-free recovery method that operates entirely
L201@P4: in weight space and is agnostic to the pruning algorithm (see Figure 3). GLUESTICK
L202@P4: requires only the original dense model and its pruned counterpart, and incurs a one-time
L203@P4: offline cost; no additional training is required.
L204@P4: Specifically, for each linear layer with dense weight matrix Wdense ∈ R
L205@P4: dout×din and its pruned
L206@P4: version Wpruned (fixed, preserving the original 2:4/4:8 pattern), we define the gap matrix:
L207@P4: Wgap = Wdense − Wpruned, (1)
L208@P4: which captures lost information due to pruning. We then compute a truncated singular
L209@P4: value decomposition (SVD) of the gap matrix:
L210@P4: Wgap = UΣV
