L241@P4: GLUESTICK preserves the efficiency gains of structured 50% sparcity.
L242@P4: We refer to our method as GLUESTICK-r to indicate the chosen value of r. We note
L243@P4: that the parameter r provides a dial between memory usage and recovery. Smaller values
L244@P4: of r favor memory savings, while larger values prioritize recovery. In practice, integrating
L245@P4: GLUESTICK into a model, requires only a wrapper around pruned layers (Appendix A).
L246@P4-5: 5Preprint, September 2025.
L247@P5: 4 Experimental Setting
L248@P5: Our experimental setup spans two benchmarks covering distinct robotics domains: manipulation and navigation. We evaluate three different VLA models on these tasks, with results
L249@P5: measured using both task performance and safety metrics.
L250@P5: 4.1 Environments
L251@P5: We list here short descriptions of our test environments (see Appendix B.1 for more details).
L252@P5: Manipulation. We evaluate on LIBERO (Liu et al., 2023), a benchmark designed to test
L253@P5: embodied manipulation skills inspired by human activities (see Figure 1, right). LIBERO
L254@P5: tasks provide agents, embodied as a Franka Panda arm, with natural language instructions
L255@P5: and visual observations of the environment. The benchmark comprises four task suites:
L256@P5: LIBERO-Spatial (same objects, varied layouts), LIBERO-Object (same layout, varied objects), LIBERO-Goal (varied task goals), and LIBERO-Long (long-horizon tasks).
L257@P5: Navigation. We evaluate navigation using the VLN-CE-Isaac benchmark (Cheng et al.,
L258@P5: 2025a), which simulates legged robots (e.g., the Unitree Go2 quadruped and the H1 humanoid) traversing indoor environments to reach goal locations (see Figure 1, left). Agents
L259@P5: receive image observations and natural language instructions that can involve long-horizon,
L260@P5: compositional reasoning (e.g., “walk toward the French doors and turn left, pass the kitchen
L261@P5: area, and wait at the end of the hallway near the painting”). The robot executes velocity
L262@P5: commands (e.g., move forward 0.75 m, turn right 15°), which are generated by a VLA model.
L263@P5: 4.2 Models
L264@P5: We list here descriptions of the VLA models studied. See Appendix B.3 for more details.
L265@P5: OpenVLA (Kim et al., 2024) is a 7B-parameter generalist VLA model for manipulation,
L266@P5: built on the LLaMA-2 7B language backbone (Touvron et al., 2023) with SigLIP (Zhai et al.,
L267@P5: 2023) and DINOv2 (Oquab et al., 2023) transformer-based vision encoders. It takes RGB
L268@P5: observations and natural language instructions as input, and autoregressively outputs a 7D
L269@P5: low-level end-effector pose along with gripper open/close commands.
L270@P5: WorldVLA (Cen et al., 2025) is a 7B manipulation-oriented VLA that emphasizes
L271@P5: long-horizon consistency through an autoregressive action world-modeling objective. It is
L272@P5: initialized from the 7B Chameleon vision–language model (Team, 2024) with a convolutionbased VQ-GAN vision encoder (Esser et al., 2021). The model ingests the current RGB
L273@P5: observation, a sequence of history images, and a natural language instruction; generating
L274@P5: 7D low-level end-effector poses along with gripper open/close commands in action chunks.
L275@P5: NaVILA (Cheng et al., 2025a) is an 8B-parameter, navigation-focused VLA designed
L276@P5: for legged robots. It is built on the VILA vision–language model (Lin et al., 2024), which
L277@P5: combines a ViT-based visual encoder with a language backbone inspired by LLaVA’s architecture (Liu et al., 2024a), but pre-trained on a unique mixture of data. The model
L278@P5: consumes the current egocentric image along with a set of history frames and natural language instructions; outputing velocity commands executed by a locomotion controller.
L279@P5: 4.3 Evaluation Metrics
L280@P5: We evaluate VLA agents on two axes: task success and safety. Success captures whether
L281@P5: the agent achieves the stated goal. Safety captures whether it does so without causing harm
L282@P5: to itself or the environment. Refer to Appendix B.2 for more detailed metric definitions.
L283@P5: Task Success. We report binary per-episode success: an episode is successful if the agent
L284@P5: completes the objective, and unsuccessful otherwise.
L285@P5: Safety. Following prior robotics safety work (Dulac-Arnold et al., 2019; Geng et al., 2023;
L286@P5: Morton & Pavone, 2025), we operationalize safety as the absence of harm caused to the
L287@P5: robot or its surroundings. For manipulation, we monitor robot- and environment-centered
