L301@P6: 4.4 Baselines
L302@P6: We consider three pruning strategies: (i) Full Sparse, where all linear components except
L303@P6: the language model head are pruned with 50% 2:4 structured sparsity using Wanda; (ii)
L304@P6: Sparse Language Backbone, where only the language backbone is pruned; and (iii) a
L305@P6: Memory-Matched Sparse Language Backbone, which prunes a subset of backbone
L306@P6: layers while maintaining 50% 2:4 sparsity in each pruned layer, to provide a fair comparison
L307@P6: to GLUESTICK’s overhead. We use strict 2:4 structured sparsity across all settings, since
L308@P6: this level of pruning is necessary to realize meaningful improvements from hardware-efficient
L309@P6: sparse kernels. We choose to use Wanda as our base pruning algorithm because it represents
L310@P6: the state of the art and is highly practical due to its minimal computational cost during
L311@P6: pruning. See Appendix C for details.
L312@P6: 5 Results
L313@P6: We structure our results around key questions, first presenting main findings on GLUESTICK then providing analysis through ablations and broader considerations of pruning.
L314@P6: 5.1 Main Results
L315@P6: Q1: Does GLUESTICK recover task performance for pruned VLAs on manipulation tasks?
L316@P6: Across all four LIBERO task suites, Full Sparse yields a severe average degradation of
L317@P6: −72.4% (Table 2 shows the average results across OpenVLA and WorldVLA). In contrast,
L318@P6: GLUESTICK–500 degrades by only −35.7%, recovering 50% of the success rate lost to pruning. Recovery is especially strong in the Spatial and Goal suites, where GLUESTICK restores 62% and 57% of lost performance, respectively. Relative to the memory-matched
L319@P6: baseline (–59.3% average LIBERO success), GLUESTICK recovers 40% of lost success,
L320@P6: substantially restoring manipulation performance while retaining pruning efficiency. This
L321@P6: demonstrates that GLUESTICK can effectively recover task performance for pruned VLAs
L322@P6: on dexterous manipulation tasks. See Appendix C.1 for details.
L323@P6: Q2: Does GLUESTICK recover task performance for pruned VLAs on navigation tasks?
L324@P6: On the VLN-CE-Isaac benchmark, the Full Sparse NaVILA model shows a −43.0% change
L325@P6: relative to the dense baseline (see Table 3). This corresponds to a collapse from 43.0%
L326@P6: success to 0%, demonstrating that pruning completely destroys navigational capability. Importantly, this failure is not a matter of taking less efficient paths—the robot’s navigation
L327@P6: behavior is fundamentally altered. After Full Sparse pruning, the mean path length increases
L328@P6: by nearly 50%, from 11.7 meters to 17.6 meters, and the mean distance to goal increases by
L329@P6: more than 40%, from 5.9 meters to 9.5 meters. Rather than approaching the goal, pruned
L330@P6: agents frequently veer into entirely different rooms (see Appendix C.2 for distributions).
