L351@P7: In contrast, GLUESTICK–500 fully restores the dense model’s performance, recovering
L352@P7: 100% of the lost success rate. Moreover, path length and final distance-to-goal remain
L353@P7: nearly identical to the dense baseline, indicating not only restored success but also restored
L354@P7: efficiency of navigation trajectories. The memory-matched baseline remains far less competitive, showing a −18% drop relative to the dense model. This highlights that for navigation,
L355@P7: GLUESTICK not only mitigates pruning degradation but completely closes the gap to dense
L356@P7: performance across both success and path-quality metrics.
L357@P7: Q3: How well does GLUESTICK restore the safety of pruned VLAs?
L358@P7: Table 3, 4 report changes in unsafe-episode rate relative to the dense baseline in navigation
L359@P7: and manipulation, respectively. Pruning increases unsafe behaviors in both domains. The
L360@P7: Full Sparse model shows the largest degradation, with unsafe episodes rising by +13.6% on
L361@P7: LIBERO and +23.0% on navigation. The Memory-Matched Sparse Backbone also increases
L362@P7: unsafe episodes by +7.5% and +8.4%, respectively.
L363@P7: By contrast, GLUESTICK–500 remains near parity with the dense policy, yielding 89% and
L364@P7: 100% fewer unsafe episodes compared to the Full Sparse model for manipulation and navigation, respectively. Overall, GLUESTICK–500 maintains the safety profile of the original
L365@P7: dense models, with only a minimal +0.4% change across domains. These results indicate
L366@P7: that GLUESTICK restores dominant weight-space directions that carry both task-relevant
L367@P7: and safety-critical signal, thereby preserving the safety of VLA models in manipulation and
L368@P7: navigation.
L369@P7: 5.2 Analysis
L370@P7: Q4: How does the rank (r) affect GLUESTICK’s recovery–memory trade-off?
L371@P7: We observe that increasing the rank r improves success-rate recovery, as shown in Figure 4,
L372@P7: but at the cost of additional memory. Table 3 illustrates this trade-off: a fully sparse
L373@P7: NaVILA model achieves the maximum memory savings of 5.74GB but suffers a −43% drop
L374@P7: in success rate. By contrast, GLUESTICK–200 recovers nearly the full dense success rate
L375@P7: while saving 5.36GB of VRAM (offering memory savings within 0.38GB of Full Sparse).
L376@P7: Thus, GLUESTICK exposes a single hyperparameter r that controls the trade off between
L377@P7: memory efficiency and task recovery. See Appendix C.1 for full GLUESTICK-200 results.
