L401@P8: Figure 4: Ablation study. Rank ablation (left) and component sensitivity (right).
L402@P8: Q5: Which VLA components are most sensitive to pruning?
L403@P8: To understand VLA component sensitivity, we selectively prune either the language backbone or the vision backbone while keeping the rest of the model dense. For OpenVLA (7.5B
L404@P8: total parameters: 89.4% in the language backbone, 9.7% in the vision backbone, and 0.9%
L405@P8: in the projector), pruning the language backbone reduces the LIBERO Spatial benchmark
L406@P8: success by −54.0%, while pruning the vision backbone reduces success by −27.8%. When
L407@P8: normalized per million parameters, pruning the vision backbone is 4.75× more damaging
L408@P8: than pruning the language backbone. We observe the same phenomenon in NaVILA (8.5B
L409@P8: total parameters: 94.5% in the language backbone, 5.0% in the vision encoder, and 0.4%
L410@P8: in the projector). Here, pruning the language backbone reduces success by −20.0%, while
L411@P8: pruning the vision backbone reduces success by −40.0%. On a per-parameter basis, vision
L412@P8: pruning is 37.5× more damaging than language pruning.
L413@P8: We find that vision backbones are disproportionately sensitive to pruning while offering little
L414@P8: memory benefit, since they comprise less than 10% of total parameters. Because pruning
L415@P8: vision components causes outsized harm relative to their limited contribution to overall
L416@P8: model size, our main evaluations include a focus on pruning the language backbone.
L417@P8: Q6: Why not compress weights directly with SVD and avoid pruning altogether?
L418@P8: A natural question is why pruning is necessary at all if weight matrices could instead be
L419@P8: compressed directly through low-rank decomposition. In principle, one could replace each
L420@P8: dense layer with an SVD approximation, storing only the top-r singular components. To test
L421@P8: this, we conducted an experiment where OpenVLA weights were approximated with a rank200 SVD (without pruning). On LIBERO Spatial, this setting achieved a 0% success rate,
L422@P8: indicating that low-rank approximations alone are insufficient to preserve the functionality
L423@P8: of VLA models. This suggests that the pruned weight matrix itself retains valuable structure
L424@P8: that cannot be captured by low-rank SVD alone. GLUESTICK leverages this by preserving
L425@P8: the pruned weights (and structured sparsity benefits) while using SVD only to reintroduce
L426@P8: lost directions, nudging the model back toward a more performant region of weight space.
L427@P8: 6 Conclusion and Future Work
L428@P8: We presented the first systematic study of pruning VLA models and showed that pruning catastrophically degrades both task success and safety. To address this, we introduced
L429@P8: GLUESTICK, a training-free, pruning-agnostic, and easily integrable post-pruning recovery
L430@P8: method that reintroduces lost directions due to strict 2:4 sparsity, restoring performance and
L431@P8: safety while retaining efficiency. Importantly, because our approach is independent of the
L432@P8: pruning algorithm, it can be applied as a universal recovery step as new pruning strategies
L433@P8: continue to emerge. Looking forward, several promising directions remain. One is to identify
L434@P8: and prioritize recovery of safety-critical directions in weight space. Another is to further
L435@P8: investigate the benefits of GLUESTICK for both inference speed and energy efficiency.
L436@P8: Finally, we hypothesize that extending GLUESTICK to select different r throughout the
L437@P8: model could further improve our results. We hope this work lays the foundation for developing compression techniques that make powerful VLA models practical for real-world
L438@P8: deployment on resource-constrained robotic platforms.
