L598@P13: Appendix
L599@P13: A GLUESTICK Details
L600@P13: Our method can be implemented in just a few lines of code.
L601@P13: Algorithm 1: PyTorch code for GLUESTICK (Offline)
L602@P13: # Compute and Store GLUESTICK Correction terms for every linear layer
L603@P13: def prime_gluestick (W_dense , W_pruned , r):
L604@P13: # W_dense: layer (l) dense weights (d_out , d_in)
L605@P13: # W_pruned: layer (l) pruned weights (d_out , d_in)
L606@P13: # r: target rank
L607@P13: W_gap = W_dense - W_pruned
L608@P13: U, S, Vh = torch.linalg.svd(W_gap)
L609@P13: U_r = U[:, :r]
L610@P13: S_r = S[:r]
L611@P13: V_r = Vh[:r, :].T
L612@P13: A = U_r * S_r. unsqueeze (0)
L613@P13: B = V_r
L614@P13: return {"A": A, "B": B}
L615@P13: In the offline stage (Algorithm 1), we iterate through the dense and pruned weights of each
L616@P13: linear layer, compute the correction terms, and store them.
L617@P13: Algorithm 2: PyTorch code for GLUESTICK (Online)
L618@P13: class GLUESTICKWrap (nn.Module):
L619@P13: def __init__(self , pruned_linear_layer , A, B):
L620@P13: super().__init__ ()
L621@P13: self. pruned_linear = pruned_linear_layer
L622@P13: self.A = A
L623@P13: self.B = B
L624@P13: def forward(self , x):
L625@P13: # Efficient Sparse MatMul
L626@P13: y = F.linear(
L627@P13: x,
L628@P13: self. pruned_linear_layer .weight ,
L629@P13: self. pruned_linear_layer .bias
L630@P13: )
L631@P13: # Compute GLUESTICK Correction
L632@P13: correction = self.A @ (self.B.T @ x)
L633@P13: return torch.add(y, correction )
L634@P13: # Load Pruned Model
L635@P13: model = load_pruned_model ()
L636@P13: # Load GLUESTICK correction terms for every linear layer
L637@P13: correction_terms = load_correction_terms ()
L638@P13: # Apply GLUESTICK to all pruned linaer layers in the model
L639@P13: model = apply_gluestick (model , correction_terms )
L640@P13: In the online stage (Algorithm 2), we load the pruned model along with the saved correction
L641@P13: terms and wrap each pruned linear layer with GLUESTICK to enable corrected inference.
L642@P13-14: 14Preprint, September 2025.
L643@P14: B Experimental Setting
L644@P14: B.1 Environments
L645@P14: Manipulation Our manipulation evaluation covers all 10 tasks from each of the four
L646@P14: LIBERO suites, with each task repeated 50 times, resulting in 2,000 total episodes.
L647@P14: Navigation VLN-CE-Isaac builds on VLN-CE (Krantz et al., 2020), which itself is based
L648@P14: on the Habitat simulator (Savva et al., 2019). Habitat provides photorealistic 3D environments and physics-based simulation for embodied AI, moving beyond the original VLN task
L649@P14: that used MatterPort3D panoramas represented as discrete navigation graphs (Anderson
L650@P14: et al., 2018). Unlike the graph-based setting, Habitat supports continuous actions and realistic perception, allowing agents to navigate freely in 3D space. However, Habitat does not
L651@P14: simulate robot embodiment—for instance, agents can move through unrealistic gaps (e.g.,
L652@P14: 10 cm between two sofas) that would be infeasible for legged robots. VLN-CE-Isaac inherits this Habitat-based formulation but extends it to physically simulated robots in Isaac
L653@P14: Sim, enabling evaluation on platforms such as the Unitree Go2 quadruped and Unitree H1
L654@P14: humanoid. This provides a comprehensive benchmark of the full navigation pipeline, from
L655@P14: high-level language understanding to low-level motor control. We evaluate on 100 randomly
L656@P14: selected scenes from the 1,077 available in the VLN-CE-Isaac benchmark.
L657@P14: Hardware In all experiments, we use an NVIDIA L40S GPU with 48 GB of VRAM.
L658@P14: B.2 Safety Definitions
L659@P14: Safety in Navigation. For navigation tasks, we use collisions as the primary safety
L660@P14: metric. A collision is recorded whenever the agent outputs actions that cause the robot to
L661@P14: make unintended contact with objects in the environment. This measures the agent’s ability
L662@P14: to move purposefully without endangering itself or its surroundings.
L663@P14: Safety in Manipulation. For manipulation tasks, we introduce a set of five safety metrics
L664@P14: that capture risks to both the robot and the environment:
L665@P14: • Joint limit violations: Occur when the agent outputs actions that drive joint
L666@P14: angles close to or beyond their mechanical limits, which can cause long-term wear
L667@P14: or physical damage to the robot’s actuators.
L668@P14: • Arm collisions: Measured when any part of the robot arm (excluding the end
L669@P14: effector) makes unintended contact with the environment, potentially harming both
L670@P14: the robot and external objects.
L671@P14: • Object velocities: We track the velocities of manipulated objects as a proxy
L672@P14: for physical stability, penalizing outcomes where objects are flung, dropped, or
L673@P14: otherwise move unsafely.
L674@P14: • End-effector containment: We enforce that the end effector remains within a
L675@P14: bounded three-dimensional workspace region. This ensures that the robot’s actions stay localized and prevents dangerous or uncontrolled motions outside of its
L676@P14: designated operating zone.
L677@P14: • Whole-body containment: Similarly, we verify that the robot’s entire body
L678@P14: remains within a global containment region. Exiting this region can represent unsafe
L679@P14: configurations or uncontrolled movement, posing risk to both the platform and its
L680@P14: environment.
L681@P14: An episode is deemed unsafe whenever it violates one or more of the defined safety metrics.
L682@P14: We use the following thresholds: joint limit violations occur if a joint exceeds 0.1% of its
L683@P14: range; object motion is unsafe if velocity exceeds 1.0 m/s; the robot body is unsafe if more
L684@P14: than 1% extends outside the containment region; and the end effector is unsafe if more than
L685@P14: 5% extends beyond containment. Containment regions are computed from the ground-truth
L686@P14: dataset.
L687@P14-15: 15Preprint, September 2025.
L688@P15: B.3 Models
L689@P15: OpenVLA. We use four officially released OpenVLA checkpoints, each fine-tuned on one
L690@P15: of the four LIBERO task suites.
L691@P15: WorldVLA. We use four officially released WorldVLA checkpoints, each fine-tuned on one
L692@P15: of the four LIBERO task suites. We ran experiments with the default action chunking of
L693@P15: 25; however, we observe that pruning leads to sometimes meaningless token outputs under
L694@P15: this setting causing invalid actions for the robot to execute. For fairness, we instead set
L695@P15: the action chunk size to 1, which increases evaluation time but provides a more reliable
L696@P15: comparison. WorldVLA also allows varying the number of history images; we adopt the
L697@P15: default configuration of one history image together with the current image.
L698@P15: C Main Results
L699@P15: For LIBERO benchmarks, models are pruned with Wanda using a 15K calibration dataset
L700@P15: drawn from the LIBERO fine-tuning corpus. For NAVILA, we use a 1K calibration dataset.
L701@P15: The Memory-Matched Sparse Backbone prunes 75% of layers for OpenVLA and WorldVLA,
L702@P15: and 81.3% of layers for NaVILA. It is important to note that in the Memory-Matched
L703@P15: setting, although only a fraction of layers are pruned, the pruned layers still maintain 50%
L704@P15: structured 2:4 sparsity. Since WorldVLA uses a convolution-based vision encoder, we do
L705@P15: not apply pruning to that component of the model.
