L782@P17: C.2 GLUESTICK Navigation Task Performance Recovery
L783@P17: Full Dense trajectories remain short and goal-directed, while Full Sparse trajectories are
L784@P17: substantially longer and terminate farther from the goal, reflecting severe degradation in
L785@P17: navigational ability (See Figure 5). In contrast, GLUESTICK–500 closely matches the Full
L786@P17: Dense distribution, indicating that it restores not only success rates but also the efficiency
L787@P17: and precision of navigation behavior.
L788@P17: D Analysis
L789@P17: D.1 Calibration Set Selection
L790@P17: Figure 6: Calibration Set Visualization. LIBERO Spatial calibration trajectories. full
L791@P17: trajectories (left) 5% window around the first gripper-closing event, with red segments marking closed-gripper states (right).
L792@P17: The influence of calibration data on pruning outcomes in robotics remains largely underexplored. To investigate its impact, we studied how the calibration set choice affects Wanda’s
L793@P17: baseline pruning performance before applying GLUESTICK. Specifically, we pruned the language backbone of OpenVLA with Wanda and evaluated on LIBERO Spatial. We provided
L794@P17: Wanda with 15K states from the LIBERO fine-tuning corpus. We then constructed a smaller
L795@P17: but more targeted calibration set consisting of 3.5K states within a 5% window around the
L796@P17: first gripper-closing event (Figure 6). Our experiments show that the more targeted calibration set improved the pruned language backbone’s success rate on LIBERO Spatial from
L797@P17: 31.2% to 33.4%, a gain of about 2%. We adopt this strategy as part of GLUESTICK.
L798@P17: Interestingly, the smaller calibration set yielded a slightly higher success rate.
L799@P17: D.2 Singular Value Selection
L800@P17: We ask whether there is an optimal criterion for selecting singular values. All results reported
L801@P17: in this paper use the top-r singular components ranked by magnitude. To test alternatives,
L802@P17: we conducted an experiment where, for GLUESTICK-200 on a fully sparse language backbone, singular values were instead chosen uniformly at random. In this setting, the model
L803@P17: recovered only ∼10% of the task success achieved by the magnitude-based selection. This
L804@P17: indicates that the choice of singular values is highly influential, with selecting the largest
L805@P17: components by magnitude playing a central role in effective recovery. However, different
L806@P17: singular values may capture complementary subspaces in weight space, and future work
L807@P17: could explore whether alternative selection criteria better preserve metrics such as safety.
L808@P17-18: 18Preprint, September 2025.
L809@P18: E Use of LLMs
L810@P18: LLMs were used to assist with grammar checking and correcting typos during the preparation of this paper.
L811@P18: 19


